WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
API design is the first line of library security.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:12.920
Callers may use the library under deadline pressure, through wrappers, across language boundaries, or with incomplete knowledge of the internal design.

3
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:20.620
A secure API should make the safe path easy, make risky behavior visible, and make accidental misuse less likely.

4
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.340
Narrow interfaces help.

5
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:31.880
A function with a clear purpose, clear input types, and a clear result is easier to review than an overloaded entry point that changes behavior based on several flags.

6
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:39.740
Strong types can communicate security-relevant choices better than plain strings, integers, or Boolean parameters.

7
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.520
Names should describe consequences, not only mechanics.

8
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:50.740
A reviewer should be able to see when an operation reads, writes, allocates, blocks, or changes trust boundaries.

9
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.420
Defaults deserve careful attention.

10
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.600
A configuration object should start in a safe, conservative state.

11
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:03.060
Optional behavior should be explicit, documented, and tied to a real use case.

12
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:09.000
If a caller must opt into a risky mode, the API should make that choice obvious in code review.

13
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:15.100
Convenience should not quietly expand authority, disable validation, or hide resource costs.

14
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:21.040
Secure defaults reduce the amount of security knowledge every caller must bring to the first use.

15
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.520
Misuse resistance is not about distrusting callers.

16
00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:28.780
It is about acknowledging how libraries are used in production systems.

17
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.320
Callers make mistakes, requirements change, and wrappers abstract details away.

18
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:43.620
Good APIs validate parameters before trusted use, preserve invariants, return actionable errors, and give callers a clear contract for sensitive operations.

19
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:48.040
The result is a library that helps consumers do the right thing by default.

20
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:54.140
If unsafe functionality is necessary, separate it clearly and explain the required preconditions.

21
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.560
Tests should show both ordinary use and rejected misuse.

22
00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:08.420
Design reviews should ask whether a new method changes authority, lifetime, or validation expectations, because those changes shape every wrapper and integration.
